    "lawCode": "Add §231, Pub Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Public Service Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S4519\n\nSPONSOR: SKOUFIS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the public service law, in relation to\nestablishing a cap on the amount a subscriber may be charged for leasing\na modem, remote or other device from a cable television company\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo establish a cap on the total cumulative cost a cable television or\nInternet service provider can charge a subscriber leasing a modem,\nremote control or other device while receiving service from that provid-\ner after such fees total the retail cost of the device.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: Establishes definitions for a \"lease\" and \"modem\" for the\npurposes of this new section of law. This section also states that the\ncable or Internet provider cannot charge any subscriber more than the\nactual cost of the modem, remote control or other device paid for by the\ncompany. In addition, this section would also codify penalties if a\ncable or internet provider is in violation of this law which can author-\nize a court to impose a civil penalty of not more than $500 for each\nviolation.\n\nSection 2: Effective Date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nCable and Internet service providers charge customers for services that\nare dependent on the use of leased equipment from the same service\nprovider, such as a modem, remote control and other device.  While some\nconsumers have the option of purchasing their own modems, many may not\nbe aware of other options and some choose to lease as part of a service\npackage.\n\nIn some cases, providers continue charging a monthly fee for use of\nthese devices long after the retail cost of each device has been reached\nor exceeded through monthly fees from the customer. This legislation\nwould limit the monthly fees assessed to subscribers at that retail cost\nof the device, established at start of service to a customer. The legis-\nlation would allow for a court to impose a civil fine of up to $500 for\neach violation.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2017-18: A3025 - Referred to Corporations, Authorities and Commissions\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
